  "worked_solution": "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Riccati differential equation with \n\n$$\na(x)={x}^{2}+1,\\,b(x)=-3,\\,c(x)=x.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=x{y}^{3}+2y-1\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation III is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}={x}^{2}y+\\frac{5}{y}.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation because it contains a $\\frac{5}{y}$ term instead of a combination involving only ${y}^{2}$, $y$, and a function of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
